          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "Now I understand the portfolio. Key facts:\n\n- Four headlines: umbra (occlusion engine / policy, the renamed successor to cli-guard, shipping `umbra` CLI), mcp-beaver (infrastructure, not product), housecast (roster engine and eval runner), acompose (renders roster bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority).\n- Wedge ranking from 2026-09-02: housecast (attested bundles wedge 1), refusal traces (sirens-echo), umbra (wedge 3), mcp-beaver (wedge 4 closed as product).\n- Kai's rule rate is $150/hour, confirmed 2026-08-24, with 70% utilisation.\n- Comp floor: $170K nonprofit/gov, $200K otherwise, $250K top anchor.\n\nNow the question: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra? Give me price bands for each, and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\"\n\nSo this is about pricing a custom engagement: a one-time customization/implementation (\"cut\") plus long-term support, for either acompose (the bundle renderer) or umbra (the occlusion/policy engine).\n\nThis is a Portfolio Director question. I need to:\n1. Understand what acompose and umbra actually DO (I have portfolio.md but it's thin on the actual product surface). Let me check lore-stack-agent-systems maybe, or the repos themselves. Actually, there might be more detail. Let me think about what I know.\n\nActually, the key constraints:\n- Never invent comp/pricing numbers without evidence. The outward personality says WebSearch before claiming expensive/cheap/novel.\n- Kai has a confirmed rule rate of $150/hr. That's the floor for any time-and-materials pricing.\n- There's lore-self-household-index which has break-even rate $137/hr and contracting rate — but I should be careful. Actually the question is about what a CUSTOMER would pay for acompose/umbra as a product service, not what Kai charges for her own contracting time. But there's overlap.\n\nLet me think about whether to WebSearch. The outward personality demands I