market. This is thinner — it's more of a \"config rendering\" layer. The market for this is weaker as a standalone product.\n\nThe \"custom one-time cut\" = a scoped custom implementation/integration. \"Long-term support\" = annual retainer.\n\nKey grounding anchors:\n- Kai's rule rate: $150/hr (confirmed). This is the floor for her time.\n- Dev tools support retainer: $1,250-5K/month (saashero), enterprise $100K-500K annual (comparetiers).\n- Agent security enterprise: custom, high.\n\nNow let me think about the price bands. But I must be disciplined: I'm not to \"invent\" numbers, but the user is explicitly ASKING me to give price bands. This is a legitimate request for a pricing estimate. The comp rules are about Kai's own salary/comp preferences, not about product pricing. The guardrail and outward personalities apply.\n\nActually, wait — there's a subtlety. The \"do not invent comp preferences\" rule is about Kai's personal compensation. Product pricing for acompose/umbra is different. But I should still ground the bands in:\n1. Kai's confirmed rule rate ($150/hr) — for the \"one-time cut\" which is her labor.\n2. Outside market comparables — for what customers pay.\n\nLet me also recall lore-self-household-index has break-even rate $137/hr and contracting rate. The saas-wedges mentions \"The steady-state bar derives from the independent rate in lore-self-compensation.\" So the $150/hr rate is the relevant one.\n\nLet me do 1-2 more searches to nail down:\n1. Styra/OPA consulting pricing or policy-as-code implementation cost.\n2. MCP gateway / agent security pricing (Lakera/Pillar pricing).\n\nThen synthesize. Let me run these in parallel.",
