egory with actual budget. So a custom cut + support of umbra has real buyers — but they're buying from funded vendors (Pillar, Lakera/Check Point, Prompt Security), not from a solo maintainer. The wedge 3 (umbra) was endorsed but there's competition: Kong AI Gateway 3.13 shipped MCP Tool ACLs, Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, Permit.io all in it (thirteen named gateways) — that killed wedge 4 (mcp-beaver) as a product. So the guardrail/occlusion space is crowded.\n\n- **acompose** (roster→bundle renderer, no executable authority): this is infrastructure/composition tooling, not a standalone market. There's no commercial category for \"renders a YAML roster into a harness bundle.\" It's a piece of a larger platform. Buyers for acompose alone are essentially nonexistent; it only sells as part of the agent-platform story (housecast + umbra + acompose together).\n\nSo the honest answer:\n- **Would anyone buy one?** \n  - umbra: maybe — there's a real category and real budget, but the buyer set that would pay a solo dev for a \"custom cut + support\" of an early OSS occlusion engine is small and mostly would either (a) adopt the OSS free and pay a vendor, or (b) hire staff. The realistic buyer is a specific team with a specific policy need who wants the author's help. This is consulting, not a product.\n  - acompose: no — not as a standalone. Zero standalone category. Only sells bundled.\n\n- **Price bands** (grounded in $150/hr rule rate + market):\n  - One-time \"cut\" (scoped custom integration): if priced on Kai's time, a scoped 2-4 week engagement. At $150/hr, a small cut = ~$12K-25K (1-3 weeks), a real integration = $40K-80K (1-2 months). But the market ceiling for a solo OSS maintainer doing custom work is lower than enterprise-vendor pricing.\n  - Long-term support: grounded in dev-tool retainers. $1.5K-5K/month early ($18K-60K/yr), scaling. Compare enterprise $100K-500K annual — but that's a funded company with SLAs, not a solo dev.\n\nLet me structure the bands concretely.\n\n**umbra — custom cut + long-term support:**\n- One-time cut: $15K-60K, depending on scope (a specific policy integration vs. an enterprise rollout). Anchor: $150/hr × 100-400 hrs. Actually let me give cleaner bands.\n- Long-term support: $2K-6K/month retainer ($24K-72K/yr), grounded in the $1.25K-5K dev-tool retainer range and the fact that umbra touches security (which commands a premium).\n\n**acompose — custom cut + long-term support:**\n- One-time cut: $5K-20K (it's a thinner layer, fewer hours; and no buyer urgency).\n- Long-term support: $1K-3K/month, only if bundled with the rest of the platform.\n- And: no standalone buyers.\n\nActually, let me be careful. The user asked me to \"give me price bands for each.\" I should give concrete bands but be honest that these are estimates grounded in (a) the $150/hr confirmed rate and (b) outside market comparables, not observed transactions (there are none).\n\nThe guardrail requires: strongest case against first, then reversal condition, then verdict.\n\nThe strongest case against my own answer (that someone would pay these bands): \n- There is no observed demand. The portfolio has zero customers, zero revenue, no flagship (confirmed in portfolio.md). The agent-security category that would buy umbra is already saturated by funded vendors (Pillar, Lakera/Check Point, Prompt Security, Operant, Noma, Lasso) plus Kong's free MCP Tool ACLs. A solo maintainer cannot compete on procurement (SOC 2, SLAs, sales motion) for the $100K+ enterprise deals, and at the low end the OSS is free to self-serve. acompose has no standalone category at all. So the realistic price a buyer actually pays is closer to $0 (self-serve OSS) or a modest consulting rate, not enterprise-vendor prices.\n- Reversal condition: evidence that would flip this — an inbound inquiry where a named team with a named policy problem asks for a paid integration, or a second operated subject (beyond Sirens Echo n-of-one) proving demand. Specifically: one real purchase order or one signed retainer.\n\nSo the honest verdict: \n- Would anyone buy one? For umbra: a narrow maybe, as custom consulting for a specific team, not as a product at enterprise prices. For acompose: no, not standalone.\n\nLet me now also decide on the decision the work supports. The user is essentially asking whether to bother offering this, and at what price. The director should name the gate: \"the gate is whether to price and list a custom-cut-plus-support offering.\"\n\nActually, let me re-read the exact ask: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra? Give me price bands for each, and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\"\n\nSo three deliverables:\n1. Price band for acompose (one-time cut + long-term support)\n2. Price band for umbra (one-time cut + long-term support)\n3. Verdict on whether anyone would buy.\n\nLet me write this decisively. Call first, then the one fact that decided it, then reversal.\n\nLet me also note I should report the WebSearch result counts (outward + director run requirements: \"report how many independent results came back\").\n\nI ran 6 searches total (3 + 3), each returning ~5 results. Independent results across the searches: roughly ~25-30 results returned, of which maybe 6-8 were distinct useful references. I'll report honestly: I checked ~30 returned results across 6 queries; the load-bearing outside facts came from a handful of independent sources (Styra/OPA support, the 2026 agent-security roundups, Lakera pricing guide, saashero retainer figures, comparetiers enterprise minimums).\n\nLet me also be precise about the \"$150/hr\" — that's Kai's confirmed rule rate, the floor for her labor in a custom cut.\n\nNow, let me think about whether I need to actually check the repos for acompose/umbra to confirm what they are. I have portfolio.md which is authoritative enough for this. The portfolio.md says: \"umbra is the occlusion engine... shipping the umbra CLI.\" \"agent-compose sits downstream and renders that bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority.\"\n\nNote: the question says \"acompose\" — portfolio uses \"agent-compose\" / \"acompose\" interchangeably. acompose = agent-compose = the bundle renderer. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: the saas-wedges note that wedge 3 is umbra (endorsed), and the \"refusal traces\" wedge is housecast/sirens-echo. The \"guarded MCP endpoint\" was wedge 4 (mcp-beaver, closed as product). So umbra's commercial prospect (policy/occlusion) is real but crowded, and the key competitive fact: \"Kong AI Gateway 3.13 shipped MCP Tool ACLs: declarative YAML, default-deny. Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive and Permit.io are all in it, thirteen named gateways.\" That's the strongest case against umbra having standalone buyers — the exact thing umbra does is being commoditized into every gateway.\n\nThis is important and I should cite it. It's from the local record (saas-wedges), which is fine to cite as the inside evidence; the outward check (Kong AI Gateway 3.13) is a public fact.\n\nLet me now also weigh: should I do an AskUserQuestion? The director run requires AskUserQuestion for choices. But the user asked a direct question for me to answer with bands and a verdict, not to make a decision for them. I'll answer directly. The question isn't asking me to pick between options for the human; it's asking for an analysis. I'll deliver the analysis decisively.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether AskUserQuestion is warranted. The run attributes say \"AskUserQuestion with a closed option set and the recommendation first, rather than prose that leaves every option open.\" But the user's question is not a multiple-choice decision; it's \"give me bands and a verdict.\" I'll answer in prose with the verdict, which is the appropriate form. I won't force an AskUserQuestion where it doesn't fit.\n\nLet me write the final answer now.\n\nStructure:\n1. **The call first** (verdict): Whether anyone buys → umbra: a narrow yes, only as custom consulting for a specific team, not as product. acompose: no, not standalone. Price bands follow.\n\n2. Strongest case against (guardrail): the honest truth that there are zero buyers today and the category is commoditized.\n\nActually, the guardrail says: \"No assessment leaves this seat without the strongest case against it written first.\" But also the director's \"how you report\" says \"The call first, then the one fact that decided it, then what would reverse it.\"\n\nThese two are slightly in tension (guardrail says case-against-first; director says call-first). Let me reconcile: I'll lead with the call, then immediately the case against as the deciding reasoning, then reversal. Actually the guardrail procedure is \"Case against, then reversal condition, then verdict.\" But the director voice says \"names the decision and its gate, before the reasoning that reached it.\"\n\nI think the right synthesis for this seat: state the decision/call up front (verdict), then present the case-against-first as the reasoning, then reversal. The guardrail's \"case against first\" means the case against appears before the supporting/positive case and before the final endorsement — not necessarily as the very first sentence. The director voice and guardrail can coexist: verdict first (director), then the case against (guardrail) as the reasoning, then reversal, then the positive grounding.\n\nActually, let me just be disciplined and follow the guardrail's ordering for the assessment portion while opening with a one-line call. Let me write it cleanly.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n---\n\n**The call:** umbra has a narrow consulting market and a floor around $150/hr of Kai's time; acompose has no standalone market. Bands below. No one pays enterprise prices to a solo maintainer for either today.\n\nThen: strongest case against, reversal, then the bands.\n\nLet me get the numbers tight.\n\nPrice bands (grounded):\n\n**umbra** (occlusion/policy engine for agent I/O):\n- One-time cut: $12K–$60K. Lower = a scoped integration (~1–2 weeks at $150/hr ≈ $12–24K). Upper = a production policy rollout with custom guardfiles + integration across a team's harness (~1–2 months ≈ $40–80K, but I'll cap the realistic solo band at $60K). Actually let me anchor cleanly: 80–400 hours × $150 = $12K–$60K.\n- Long-term support: $1.5K–$5K/month ($18K–$60K/yr). Grounded in the $1.25K→$3–5K dev-tool retainer ladder, with a premium because security touches production and SLAs.\n\n**acompose** (roster→bundle renderer):\n- One-time cut: $4K–$20K (a thin rendering layer; 25–130 hours × $150).\n- Long-term support: $1K–$2.5K/month ($12K–$30K/yr), and only as part of the wider platform, not standalone.\n\n**Would anyone buy?**\n- umbra: a narrow maybe — one specific team with one specific policy/occlusion problem, buying the author's help. The category has real budget (agent security is funded), but the budget goes to funded vendors (Pillar, Lakera/Check Point, Prompt Security, Operant, Noma, Lasso) and the self-serve OSS is free; and Kong 3.13 + Red Hat + Cerbos + Permit.io + ToolHive already ship the MCP-tool-ACL version of this. So the realistic buyer is a consulting client, not a product customer.\n- acompose: no, standalone. It has no category; it only sells bundled into the four-headline platform story.\n\n**Strongest case against** (before I endorse these bands): There are zero observed transactions. The portfolio has no customers, no flagship (portfolio.md, confirmed). The agent-security budget that umbra would tap is already routed to funded vendors with SOC 2/SLAs/sales — a solo maintainer can't clear that procurement bar for the $100K+ enterprise deals, and below that the OSS is free. acompose has no standalone category at all; \"renders a YAML roster into a harness bundle\" is a feature inside a platform, not a product someone shops for. So the honest center of the distribution is $0 (self-serve OSS), and the bands above describe what a motivated single client might pay, not a market.\n\n**Reversal condition:** one signed PO or retainer from a named team with a named policy need (and, for acompose, any standalone inquiry at all). Or a second operated subject beyond Sirens Echo's n-of-one. Until then these bands are a pricing hypothesis, not a price list.\n\n**Outside check (count):** I ran 6 searches returning ~30 results; the load-bearing outside facts came from ~7 independent pages: Styra/OPA support (third-party policy consulting exists, no public price), the 2026 agent-security roundups (Toolradar, integrate.io, cybersecuritynews — category is funded, Pillar is a Gartner Guardian Agent representative), Lakera pricing guide (Community $0/mo, then demo/enterprise quote), accuro \"What AI Security Actually Costs in 2026\" (no vendor publishes a first-party price — all demo-gated), saashero (dev-tool support $1.25K→$3–5K/month), comparetiers (enterprise dev tools $100K–500K annual minimum).\n\n---\n\nThat's thorough. Let me tighten and make sure I hit all the requirements:\n- Call first ✓\n- Case against first (in the assessment body) ✓\n- Reversal condition ✓\n- Outside check with result count ✓\n- Price bands for each (one-time + support) ✓\n- Verdict on whether anyone buys ✓\n- Guardrail: no manufactured consensus/customers/revenue ✓ (I'm explicitly saying zero customers)\n- Don't invent Kai comp ✓ (I'm using the confirmed $150/hr rate as the labor floor, not inventing a preference)\n- Decisive: close the option set ✓ (acompose = no standalone; umbra = consulting only)\n\nOne nuance: the $150/hr is Kai's rule rate for her own contracting, confirmed 2026-08-24. I can use it as the floor for \"a custom one-time cut\" because that IS her labor. That's legitimate — it's not inventing; it's the confirmed rate. I'll note it's the confirmed rule rate.\n\nAlso note: 70% utilisation was chosen alongside the $150/hr. I don't need that detail here.\n\nLet me also make sure I'm not overclaiming