          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The call\n\n**acompose: no standalone buyer. umbra: a narrow maybe, and only as custom consulting — not as a product at enterprise prices.** The price bands below are a pricing hypothesis grounded in Kai's confirmed $150/hr rule rate and outside market comparables, not observed transactions. There are zero observed transactions today.\n\n## Bands\n\n**umbra** (the occlusion/policy engine — successor to cli-guard)\n- One-time cut: **$12K–$60K** (80–400 hours at $150/hr). Low end is a scoped policy integration; high end is a production rollout with custom guardfiles wired into one team's harness.\n- Long-term support: **$1.5K–$5K/month** ($18K–$60K/yr). Anchored to the dev-tool retainer ladder of $1.25K → $3–5K/month, with a premium because it touches production security.\n\n**acompose** (renders a roster YAML into harness bundles, no executable authority)\n- One-time cut: **$4K–$20K** (25–130 hours). It's a thin rendering layer; there is no buyer urgency behind it.\n- Long-term support: **$1K–$2.5K/month**, and only as part of the four-headline platform, never standalone.\n\n## Whether anyone buys\n\n- **umbra — narrow maybe.** The category has real money in it: agent security/MCP gateways is funded (Pillar, Lakera now under Check Point, Prompt Security, Operant, Noma, Lasso), and Pillar is a Gartner \"Guardian Agent\" representative. But that budget routes to funded vendors with SOC 2, SLAs, and a sales motion a solo maintainer can't clear for the $100K+ enterprise deals — and below that, the OSS is free to self-serve. Kong AI Gateway 3.13 already shipped MCP Tool ACLs (declarative YAML, default-deny), with Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, and Permit.io in the same space. So the realistic buyer is one specific team with one specific policy problem, paying the author for help. That is consulting, not a product.\n- **acompose — no.** \"Renders a YAML roster into a harness bundle\" is a feature inside the platform, not a category anyone shops for. It only sells bundled.\n\n## Strongest case against (before I endorse any of this)\n\nThe honest center of the distribution is **$0** — self-serve OSS. The portfolio has no customers, no flagship, no revenue (portfolio.md, confirmed 2026-08-28), and the agent-security budget umbra would tap is already spoken for by funded vendors plus Kong's free ACLs. The bands above describe what a motivated single client *might* pay, not a market. Pricing them is not the same as anyone standing behind them.\n\n**Reversal condition:** one signed PO or retainer from a named team with a named policy need — and for acompose, any standalone inquiry at all — or a second operated subject beyond Sirens Echo's n-of-one. Until then these are a hypothesis, not a price list.\n\n**Outside check (count):** six searches, ~30 results returned. The load-bearing facts came from seven independent pages — Styra/OPA support (third-party policy consulting exists, no first-party price published), the 2026 agent-security roundups (Toolradar, integrate.io, cybersecuritynews), Lakera's pricing guide (Community $0/mo, then demo/enterprise quote), accuro's \"What AI Security Actually Costs in 2026\" (no vendor publishes a first-party price — all demo-gated), saashero (dev-tool support $1.25K→$3–5K/mo), and comparetiers (enterprise dev tools $100K–500K annual minimum)."
